  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nThis Afternoon-Tonight...\n\nA developing low is centered near the NE-SD border. An\nassociated warm front is lifting north across the area, \ncurrently located roughly along a line from Grand Island to \nGeneva. A cold front is soon to move into northwestern portions \nof the area currently located just west of a line from Thedford-\nNorth Platte-McCook. In the warm sector of this low (between \nwarm and cold fronts) breezy southerly winds are helping to \nadvect warm moist air into the area. Temperatures in the warm \nsector are in the 80s and 90s, warmest across north central \nKansas. Elevated showers/weak storms are ongoing across the area\nbut overall remain fairly brief/light. \n\nAs the cold front continues to push across the area, storms are \nexpected to develop along and ahead of the cold front during the \nlate afternoon-early evening hours. CAPE values of 2000+J/Kg \ncombined with 35kts of shear would support strong-severe \nthunderstorms. Initial storm development is favored to be discrete \nwhich supports a supercellular storm mode. Discrete/Supercellular \nstorms would carry a potential for all hazards (Large hail, damaging \nwind gusts, isolated tornado). While an isolated tornado can't be \nruled out along/east of Highway 81 the best chances look to be just \neast of the forecast area, closest to the warm front, where surface \nwinds maintain a more due southerly direction. Over time, storms \nwill become more widespread along the front forming into a \ncluster/line of storms.  As storms form into a cluster/line later \nthis evening, hail/tornado threat lessens, with the primary concern \nbecoming damaging wind gusts (gusts to 60-70mph). Strong-severe \nstorms exit the area around midnight. Behind the front, scattered \nnon-severe showers/storms are expected to persist throughout the \novernight hours. The steadiest rain overnight will be along and just \nnorth of the front as it continues to slide southeast, exiting the \narea around sunrise on Tuesday. \n\nTuesday...\n\nLingering showers/storms continue across southeastern portions of \nthe area on Tuesday. Cooler weather is expected on Tuesday with \nhighs in the 70s and the coldest temperatures across SE portions of \nthe area where cloud coverage is the most persistent. More \nwidespread showers/storms develop during the afternoon hours. With \nthe front located across central/southern KS, PoPs should be limited \nto mainly SE portions of the forecast area. PoP chances and \ncoverage increase further Tuesday night as the LLJ results in \nscattered-widespread storms across the area though severe storms\nlook unlikely at this time. \n\nWednesday...\n\nThe stalled front across Kansas begins to lift back north on \nWednesday ahead of the next passing upper level disturbance. Highs \nwill be in the 70s across Nebraska and in the low-mid 80s across \nnorth central Kansas (warmest along/south of the front). Dry weather \nis favored during the daytime hours, with rain chances returning to \nnorthwestern portions of the area during the overnight hours. \n\nThursday Onwards...\n\nSouthwesterly flow-weak ridging builds over the area Thursday and \nFriday with temperatures climbing into the 80s and 90s. As a series \nof disturbances traverse this flow aloft, it will bring continued \noff and on chances for precipitation each day mainly during the \nevening-overnight hours. Cooler weather returns this weekend as the \naforementioned disturbances flatten and push the ridge east of the \narea.",
